<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Brecknock</h1><p class="st-meta">1093 CE – 1536 CE (approx.) · capital Brecon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A marcher lordship in the Usk valley created by Bernard de Neufmarche's conquest of the Welsh kingdom of Brycheiniog.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-de-neufmarche">Bernard de Neufmarche</a> invaded the kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brycheiniog">Brycheiniog</a> in the late 11th century and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Kingdom of Deheubarth</a> near Brecon in 1093. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecon-castle">Brecon Castle</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecon-cathedral">Brecon Priory</a>, granting lands to his followers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brycheiniog">Brecknock</a> was governed as a marcher lordship with its own courts and military obligations, a Norman lowland lordship over a largely Welsh upland population. It passed by marriage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braose-family">de Braose family</a> and then to the de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohun-family">Bohuns</a>, earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>, and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Staffords</a>, dukes of Buckingham.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-stafford-3rd-duke-of-buckingham">Edward Stafford</a>, Duke of Buckingham, was executed in 1521 and the lordship came to the crown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-in-wales-acts-1535-and-1542">Laws in Wales Act</a> of 1536 made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brycheiniog">Brecknock</a> a shire, which survives as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Powys</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1093 CE – 1536 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.45%;width:8.82%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1093 CE – 1536 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brecon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernard de Neufmarche, William de Braose, Humphrey de Bohun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh, Anglo-Norman French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brecon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=lordship-of-brecknock" aria-label="Lordship of Brecknock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Brecknock lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4924 638L4924 638M4924 638L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4924 638h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brecon. <a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=lordship-of-brecknock">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
